Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** — it hosts your app in-memory with a real HTTP pipeline but no network listener.

### Setup

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing
```

### Example test

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using System.Text.Json;

public class McpIntegrationTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpIntegrationTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Initialize_ReturnsServerCapabilities()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 1,
            method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test-client", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        Assert.Equal("2025-03-26",
            body.GetProperty("result")
                .GetProperty("protocolVersion")
                .GetString());
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ToolsList_ReturnsRegisteredTools()
    {
        // Initialize first (stateful session may require it)
        await InitializeSession();

        var request = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 2, method = "tools/list" };
        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        var tools = body.GetProperty("result").GetProperty("tools");
        Assert.True(tools.GetArrayLength() > 0);
    }

    private async Task InitializeSession()
    {
        var init = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 0, method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };
        await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", init);
    }
}
```

### Key points

- **`WebApplicationFactory<Program>`** boots your full middleware pipeline (auth, routing, MCP endpoints) in-process — no ports, no containers.
- **`CreateClient()`** returns an `HttpClient` wired to the in-memory server.
- If your server uses **SSE transport**, read the response stream with `ReadAsStreamAsync()` and parse `text/event-stream` lines. For **Streamable HTTP**, a normal `POST`/response works as shown above.
- Override services (e.g., stub a database) via `factory.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))`.
- Make sure `Program` is accessible — add `public partial class Program { }` to your server's `Program.cs` if needed.
